{
 "schema": 1,
 "slug": "crowded-lora",
 "title": "Sensors on one LoRa channel",
 "question": "How many battery sensors can share one LoRa channel before the network stops delivering?",
 "headlines": [
  {
   "value": "2",
   "label": "sensors at adverts every 60 s",
   "detail": "most that still deliver \u226595% of readings"
  },
  {
   "value": "10",
   "label": "sensors at adverts every 30 min",
   "detail": "capacity grows only with the square root of the advert interval"
  },
  {
   "value": "1.3x",
   "label": "more adverts than a quiet channel would carry",
   "detail": "lost adverts reset the routers to their fastest rate"
  }
 ],
 "summary": "Each sensor sends one short reading every 2 minutes, which by itself is a trivial load. The limit comes from the mesh itself. Every routing advert is re-broadcast by every node on the channel, so N sensors cost N\u00b2 adverts per round. LoRa radios don't listen before they talk, so readings collide with that advert traffic, and the collisions bite before EU rules' 1% duty cycle does. With adverts every 60 s the channel delivers \u226595% of readings for 2 sensors. Slowing adverts to every 30 minutes lifts that to 10, but only by about the square root of the slowdown, because the advert load grows with N\u00b2. Contention also feeds on itself. A lost advert reads to its receivers as a changed route, which resets them to their fastest advertising rate, so a busy channel carries about 1.3x the adverts a quiet one would. That is why the simulated capacity lands below the closed-form ceiling (chart 4). Use the arithmetic as an upper bound and the simulation for sizing. Slower adverts also mean slower failover, as the relay scenario measures.",
